# A module whose loop variable shares a name with its own global, and with the
# program's — ouroboros#141.
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FIXTURE is the renamer's scope handling, which was wrong
# twice in one round and silently both times.
#
#   v1  dropped a name from the rename map if it was bound ANYWHERE in a
#       function body. `ctr` is a module global at one statement and a `for`
#       variable three lines later, so the global lost its rename and wrote the
#       PROGRAM's `ctr`: VM 3/1/100 against AOT 3/0/2, rc 0, zero-byte log —
#       exactly the bug the collision guard had been deleted to remove.
#   v2  renamed binders to the SAME string as module globals, so the loop
#       variable clobbered the global it shared a name with: AOT 3/2/100.
#
# Binders now take `<M>__bnd__<name>`, distinct from a global's `<M>__<name>`,
# and shadowing is occurrence-scoped: `local` shadows from its own statement
# onward, a `for` variable binds its body only, a `try` binder its catch body
# only.
#
# Three separate things must hold at once, which is why all three are printed:
# the module's function returns the right value, the module's own global is
# untouched by its loop, and the program's identically-named global survives.

ctr is 100
import _min_shadow
print of (_min_shadow.bump of 3)
print of (_min_shadow.get of [])
print of ctr
